Yes. In that framing, the face is one anchor among many: name, body, clothes, titles, reputation, possessions, biography, roles, even the stories you tell about yourself.

They are handles the world can grab, and also handles the mind uses to maintain a stable model of “me.” They matter because they coordinate identity across time and across other people, but none of them alone exhausts the person.

The deeper distinction is between identity markers and identity-generating process. Markers are relatively static: “this is my face, my name, my rank, my house.” The process is dynamic: remembering, choosing, relating, updating, acting, interpreting. Strip away enough adornments and the question becomes much sharper: what continues to generate the sense of being this particular self?

And there’s a useful warning embedded in it: a handle can become mistaken for the thing it points to. Reputation becomes self. Appearance becomes self. Job becomes self. Then damage to the handle feels like annihilation, even when the underlying process is still there.

So perhaps the ego is partly a bundle of addressable anchors around a moving center. The anchors let the center be found, named, remembered, and socially instantiated—but they are not quite the center itself.